v9k/ -- everything the Victor 9000 port needs that is not compiled into
CKERMITW.EXE.  Nothing here is built by victorow.mak and nothing here is
upstream C-Kermit.  The top-level tools/ and tests/ directories ARE
upstream (a musl build script, a pytest suite) and are not to be used for
port material; that is why this tree exists under its own name, which is
the same v9k prefix the port's code and counters already use.

This was ".probe/" until 2026-08-09.  The leading dot hid it from ls and
from anyone browsing a checkout, while PORTING.md cited it two dozen times
and CLAUDE.md's hard rule 4 REQUIRED running one of its scripts.  A
directory that a build rule depends on is not a scratch directory.  The
split below is the other half of that repair: three kinds of thing had
accumulated under one name that fitted only the third.


tools/          Standing instruments.  Run these; do not treat them as
                disposable.  See v9k/tools/README.

    mzsize.py       What an MZ image needs at load, and the smallest
                    Victor that can run it.  CLAUDE.md hard rule 4
                    requires this before changing SBSIZ/RBSIZ/MAXSP/MAXRP
                    -- "measure the image, not DGROUP".
    pktstat.py      Counts a C-Kermit packet log: packets, retransmissions
                    (uppercase S-), timeouts (<timeout>), longest packet,
                    and the types actually received.  PORTING.md SS16l.
    mapoffset.py    Turns a byte offset from the v9k: counters into "which
                    packet, and was it a resend", by adding up wire
                    lengths from the host's packet log.  This is what
                    settled the rxpeak question; PORTING.md SS16m.

proofs/         Host programs that PORTING.md SS8 cites as the correctness
                argument for a shipped change.  These are evidence, not
                probes: if one of them is unfindable or unrunnable, the
                edit it justifies is unverifiable.  "make -C v9k/proofs"
                builds and runs all of them.  See v9k/proofs/README.

    vcrc16.c        Proves the VICTOR9K arm of chk3() computes the same
                    CRC as upstream's.  Upstream edit 17, PORTING.md
                    SS16af.
    vburst.c        Proves the ISR's burst-detection arithmetic separates
                    the two defects it was built to tell apart.  The MAME
                    harness cannot reach that code at all.  PORTING.md
                    SS16q.

probes/         Genuine one-shots: a short program that answered one libc
                or DOS question and is kept only so the answer stays
                checkable.  Build lines are in the comment at the top of
                each file.  These ARE disposable in the sense the old
                README meant -- nothing depends on them.

    vwild.c         What MS-DOS 3.1 does with ".", trailing separators,
                    FindFirst and opendir.  SS16f.
    vmatch.c        ckmatch() and stat() on the exact questions the
                    wildcard path depends on.  SS16f.  (Built against the
                    retired gcc build; kept for its answer, not its build
                    line.)
    vaccess.c       What access() returns for a FAT root.  SS12.
    vfmode.c        Whether _fmode reaches the DOS runtime.  SS16h.
    vfmodefp.c      Killed the 8087-emulator hypothesis under it.  SS16h.
    vasm.c          What Open Watcom's __interrupt prologue actually
                    emits, which is why ckvisr.asm exists.  SS16t.
    vmem.c          What a running machine says it has free.  SS16x.
    macspeed.c      Sets a nonstandard rate on a Mac serial port through
                    IOSSIOSPEED, which termios cannot express.  SS11a0.
